Date: Wed, 19 Jan 1994 04:47:17 -0800 (PST)
From: szabo@netcom.com (Nick Szabo)
Subject: Product contest

Fire at me with questions, comments, critique,
suggested revisions.  My idea is a Scientist's Induction
Workbench geared towards large data sets, primarily targetted
to scientists, and secondarily to engineers and
finance/econometric modellers.

Combine the following:

* simple database with the ability to read in large numerical data streams 
quickly (Mike Korns, mfkorns@netcom.com, has written great Mac code along 
these lines).  Read popular scientific formats.
* symbolic regression (error-fitness genetic programming).  This
technique finds arbitrary nonlinear mathematical models that 
best fit numerical data sets.  Straighforward GP code and simple user 
interface for selecting function set and terminal set (data) from database, 
and a monitor showing progress and intermediate results.
* easy transport of resulting models to Mathematica for simplification,
graphing, and further analysis.
* easy transport to statistical analysis package (or include
some features of such a package in this prodcut)

Other possible features:
* [optional] Lisp code or Mathematica macros for simplifying models
(if Mathematica doesn't do a sufficiently good job with this)
* [version 2] easy tranport to spreadsheets (Lotus and Excel),
and commercialize Mike Korn's spreadsheet compiling
code (speeds up Excel spreadsheets by c. factor of 100)
* [version 2] include and check scientific units (coinidentally there
is a debate on the genetic-programming@cs.stanford.edu list
right now, about how strict such unit-checking should be)
* [version 2] Automatically Defined Functions (ADF's), advanced GP
feature that gives more parsimonious models on larger data sets
* [version 3] customized versions with features and optimizations for 
specific scientific domains, and versions specifically geared towards
engineering, econometrics, and finance.
* [version 3] extend GP to generate other
scientifically relevant models, for example chains of 
scientific reasoning that best fit large multivariate data sets,
mathematical models linked by cause-and-effect graphs, and
classification.  (Koza & Rice have done some good work
evolving models that classify proteins based on their amino
acids sequence).

Caveat: John Koza (koza@cs.stanford.edu) has a patent on
GP so you might need to pay him royalties.  However, Hugo de Geris 
(degaris@hip.atr.co.jp) has implemented symbolic regression without 
GP and you might be able to use his algorithms instead.  GP symbolic 
regression code is available in C, C++, and Lisp; the major work involved 
in porting is probably implementing good memory management (also needed 
for handling large data sets for other purposes).

The package would be geared towards hi-end Macs and PowerPCs with
plenty of memory (>15 MB, the more the merrier).

The version 2 features, and the many other features a scientist might 
want would take much more than 2 months work, but a very useful core of 
GP, large data set handling, and transport to other Mac programs is likely
doable in less, depending on the difficulty of Mac memory 
management.  

> 3.  Once completed, I must be able to market the product by word of
> mouth alone.

I hope this includes product announcements to scientists on the Internet;
this is probably an extremely effective way to market. 

I'm not sure how this would be priced, but pricing at the high end
(>$1,000) and marketing it to big science and engineering labs might be
a great way to go.  Use your contacts there, and perhaps also
sell the package as part of your consulting work.  Part of the
marketing should include identifying specific scientific problems 
and data sets most amenable to modelling via symbolic regression,
and perhaps performing such modelling in a consulting role.

