Date: Wed, 19 Jan 94 13:56:25 GMT
From: jrk@sys.uea.ac.uk (Richard Kennaway)
Subject: Extropian love

Vince Kerchner writes:
>"If we can agree that a rational system of analysis (perhaps similar
>to theorem proof in formal systems) is the basis for making decisions
>about relationships and the boundaries between relationships, and that
>the Extropian Principles (whether one agrees with them or not) and all
>propositions which can be derived from them are an example of such a
>rational system of analysis, then how can fundamentally non-rational
>constructs such as emotions, and the emotion of love (or, for some of
>you, the emotion of hate) in particular, be integrated/accomodated in
>such a system?"

I see no more difficulty in principle in rational thought about emotions
than in rational thought about any other feature of the world, such as
rocks, stars, or superconductors. According to an article in New Scientist
a few years ago, there has been some scientific work on classifying
emotions and on understanding their functions. (I believe the subject came
up here a while ago, and I quoted some references then.)

There may be difficulties in practice, due to the fact that emotions, to a
greater extent than rocks etc., can induce diminished cognitive functioning
among the people studying them, but that's just an occupational hazard. Cf.
research on psychoactive drugs or on politically charged issues. Even
geologists are sometimes injured by rocks falling on their heads.

It appears that there is a standard and quite small repertoire of basic
emotions, found in humans world-wide, though expressed in very different
ways in different cultures.  Each is evoked by certain type of situation,
and one may conjecture how the physiological responses accompanying the
emotion may have conferred a selective advantage.

As for the accomodation of emotions within a framework such as Vince
describes, it might be interesting to study the implications for the
Extropian Principles of human emotional structure, just as one may consider
the implications of nanotech, smart drugs, strong cryptography, or other
such technological developments. The Extropian Principles document already
includes appeals to certain emotions and criticism of others, but does not
systematically address the entire topic.

