Date: Wed, 19 Jan 1994 08:16:22 -0600 (CST)
From: derek@cs.wisc.edu (Derek Zahn)
Subject: Product contest

Nick Szabo:

> My idea is a Scientist's Induction Workbench geared towards large data sets

This is an interesting idea; since I spent years studying induction
techniques including GP, the thought is quite attractive to me;
unfortunately, I have no idea whether anybody would pay for it!

> Caveat: John Koza (koza@cs.stanford.edu) has a patent on
> GP so you might need to pay him royalties.  However, Hugo de Geris 
> (degaris@hip.atr.co.jp) has implemented symbolic regression without 
> GP and you might be able to use his algorithms instead.

Remember that GP is just one way of organizing a search through
"program space", or in this case "expression space".  I'd be
interested to see the patent, because there are lots of similar
ways that one could organize such a search that would have
similar results.  But I suppose that designing and validating
new symbolic regression algorithms would violate the two month
requirement.  Get some grad student taking a machine learning
course to do that grunt work as a class project!

Something else to consider is the possibility of using other
methods:  ID3 does a sort of symbolic regression, there are
variants of neural nets (logic nets, I think they're called)
that build logical expressions to classify data, there are
some (but not many) techniques for simplifying neural nets
to extract the "essence" of what they represent (and error
functions that encourage "localist" representations that
facilitate such simplification), Inductive Logic Programming
could be quite useful, and there are of course a whole
suite of statical nonlinear regression methods that could
be included in the toolbox.  Infinite room for expansion!

derek

