Alan Barksdale wrote:

> I seem to remember reading that in one of Hayek's last books he uses a notion
> of survival of the fittest social customs to help justify his opposition to
> "constructivism", i.e., believing that one can invent some theory that allows
> one to reconstruct a society from scratch.

Yes, Hayek was a believer in _evolved systems_. In _emergent
properties_, not necessarily deducible from simple axioms (at least
not in a way we can find, or have found so far).

Hayek's last book--I forget the name--was sort of muddled, from my
quick scan of it (and the opinions of Hayek fanatics like Phil Salin
and Mark Miller, as I recall). There's nothing better than "Law,
Legislation, and Liberty," especially Volume 1.

An example of this evolutionary outlook might be the common social
views on why eating pigs is OK but eating dogs is not. Hayek would
argue that thousands of years of cultural evolution, at least in the
West, have led to the current situation, that no set of basic axioms
(such as a Randite might have) will lead to the conclusion that "pigs
are food, lambs are food, dogs are not food, cats are not food").

Connections to "moral relativism" are left to the reader to think about.

